TASK: Assess whether end-to-end task success or per-call systems metrics is the better lever for separating model quality from systems performance in a benchmark. Use the published results and the measured series, and say which evidence decides it.
ROUNDS: 2
SUMMARY: The research task is to assess whether end-to-end task success or per-call systems metrics is the better lever for separating model quality from systems performance in a benchmark. The sources of information include web searches, paper searches, and data analysis, but no fact-checking was performed.

The web search provided general information related to the assessment of model quality and systems performance in benchmarks. However, it did not provide specific evidence to support either end-to-end task success or per-call systems metrics as the better lever for separating model quality from systems performance.

The paper search yielded relevant papers on the topic, but the findings were not detailed in the provided data. The data analysis provided a metric series based on published results, which could be used to compare the two metrics. However, the specific results and conclusions from the data analysis are not included in the provided data.

In summary, the web and paper searches provided general information and references, but no concrete evidence was found to decisively support either end-to-end task success or per-call systems metrics as the better lever for separating model quality from systems performance. The data analysis metric series could be used to draw a conclusion, but the specific results are missing.
